    <main class="container py-8">
        <section id="homeassistant-overview" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Home Assistant: Your Open Source Home Automation Hub</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                Home Assistant is a free and open-source software for home automation that puts local control and privacy first. It can be accessed via a web-based user interface, companion apps for Android and iOS, or voice commands using a supported virtual assistant. Home Assistant supports a vast number of devices and services, allowing you to centralize control of your smart home.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                As part of the PiSelfhosting project, Home Assistant can serve as the brain of your smart home, enabling powerful automations and seamless integration with other services, including your Frigate NVR and Mosquitto MQTT broker.
            </p>
        </section>

        ---

        <section id="homeassistant-functionality" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Detailed Functionality</h2>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Key Features:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Extensive Integrations:</strong> Supports thousands of devices and services, from smart lights and thermostats to media players and security systems.</li>
                <li><strong>Automation Engine:</strong> Create powerful automations based on triggers (e.g., motion detection), conditions (e.g., time of day), and actions (e.g., turn on a light).</li>
                <li><strong>Local Control:</strong> Prioritizes local control over cloud-based solutions, enhancing privacy and responsiveness.</li>
                <li><strong>Customizable Dashboard (Lovelace):</strong> Design your own beautiful and functional user interfaces to control your devices.</li>
                <li><strong>Energy Monitoring:</strong> Track and visualize your home's energy consumption.</li>
                <li><strong>Voice Control:</strong> Integrate with Google Assistant, Amazon Alexa, or Home Assistant's own voice assistant.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">PiSelfhosting Integration & Best Practices:</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                While a specific <code class="code">docker-compose.yml.template</code> for Home Assistant might not be directly included in the core PiSelfhosting scripts, it is a highly recommended addition that integrates seamlessly with your existing self-hosting infrastructure:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                 <li><strong>Recommended Docker Compose Setup (Example):</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-700">You would typically add a service similar to this within your unified <code class="code">docker-compose.yml</code> or as a separate compose file:</p>
                    <div class="code-block">
<pre>
services:
  homeassistant:
    container_name: piselfhosting-homeassistant
    image: ghcr.io/home-assistant/home-assistant:stable
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "8123:8123" # Home Assistant web UI
    volumes:
      - homeassistant_config:/config
      - /etc/localtime:/etc/localtime:ro
    environment:
      - TZ=Europe/Amsterdam
    networks:
      - piselfhosting_net

volumes:
  homeassistant_config:
    name: piselfhosting-homeassistant-config
</pre>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-700">Remember to include <code class="code">piselfhosting_net</code> as an external network at the bottom of your main <code class="code">docker-compose.yml</code>.</p>
                </li>
                <li><strong>Frigate Integration (Crucial for NVR Use Case):</strong>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                        The integration between Home Assistant and Frigate is incredibly powerful, forming a robust NVR and automation system.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li><strong>MQTT Auto-Discovery:</strong> Home Assistant can automatically discover Frigate entities (cameras, motion sensors, object detectors) via your Mosquitto MQTT broker. Ensure you have the MQTT integration set up in Home Assistant and configure Frigate to publish to your Mosquitto instance.</li>
                        <li><strong>Frigate Lovelace Card:</strong> There is a dedicated Frigate HACS (Home Assistant Community Store) integration that provides a rich Lovelace card for viewing live streams, recordings, and events directly in your Home Assistant dashboard.</li>
                        <li><strong>Automations:</strong> Use Frigate's object detection events (published via MQTT) as triggers for Home Assistant automations. For example:
                            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 mt-1 text-gray-700">
                                <li>Turn on outdoor lights when a "person" is detected by a specific camera.</li>
                                <li>Send a notification to your phone with a snapshot when a package is delivered.</li>
                                <li>Trigger a siren if an "animal" is detected in a restricted zone.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-gray-700"><a href="https://docs.frigate.video/integrations/home-assistant/" target="_blank">Refer to Frigate's Home Assistant Integration Documentation</a> for detailed setup steps.</p>
                </li>
                <li><strong>Mosquitto (MQTT) Integration:</strong> Home Assistant uses MQTT extensively for integrating with various smart devices and services. By having Mosquitto set up as part of PiSelfhosting, Home Assistant can leverage it as a central messaging broker for many of your IoT devices.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Configuration Details:</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                Home Assistant's primary configuration is done through its web interface. However, its persistent data lives in a Docker volume.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Configuration Path:</strong> The main configuration directory for Home Assistant is mapped to the Docker volume <code class="code">piselfhosting-homeassistant-config</code>. Within the container, this is typically <code class="code">/config</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Initial Setup:</strong> After its first launch, you will access Home Assistant's web UI (e.g., <code class="code">http://[your-pi-ip]:8123</code>) to create an initial user, discover devices, and set up integrations.</li>
                <li><strong>YAML Configuration:</strong> For advanced configurations, automations, and scripts, you will directly edit YAML files within the <code class="code">/config</code> directory (accessible via the Docker volume). Consider using tools like Samba (if installed) or Portainer's file browser to access this volume from your network.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="homeassistant-troubleshooting" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Troubleshooting</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                If you encounter issues with Home Assistant:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Check Home Assistant logs:</strong> Use <code class="code">docker logs piselfhosting-homeassistant</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Verify Port Accessibility:</strong> Ensure port 8123 is open and not in use.</li>
                <li><strong>Network Connectivity:</strong> Confirm Home Assistant can communicate with other services (e.g., Mosquitto at <code class="code">mosquitto:1883</code>) on the <code class="code">piselfhosting_net</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Configuration Validation:</strong> If manually editing YAML files, use Home Assistant's "Configuration validation" tool (Developer Tools -> YAML) before restarting.</li>
                <li><strong>Official Documentation & Community:</strong> Home Assistant has a vast and active community. Their <a href="https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/" target="_blank">official documentation</a> and forums are invaluable resources.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
